  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>The genus Curculionichthys was established only in 2015 to accommodate dwarf sucker catfishes of the subfamily Hypoptopomatinae (formerly: Otothyrinae) of Loricariidae, which were previously placed in the genus Hisonotus. The genus name translates as \"fish with a long snout\". The fish imported under the name C. sabaji are only about 1.5-2.5 cm long. The maximum length of C. sabaji known so far is 23.6 mm without caudal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>A total of 14 species have since been assigned to the genus, but none of these have appeared in the aquarium hobby to date. With 269 described species the subfamily Hypoptopomatinae is also very confusing. Many species look very similar to each other, and identification is mostly possible only for trained specialists. It therefore cannot be excluded that the fish imported under the name C. sabaji turn out to be something else, but purely visually they agree very well with C. sabaji.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>Curculionichthys sabaji originates from the drainage of the Rio Xingu.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>In nature Curculionichthys species are found in flowing water; they can be found in shallow shore areas on sand and gravel, as well as in brush hanging into the water or between water plants.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>The tiny fishes keep well. Experience with similarly small Paraotocinclus species has shown that such dwarfs should be provided with aquariums as old as possible, with a rich micro growth on which they feed. Dead leaves, root wood etc. should therefore be abundant.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>A water temperature of 25-30°C is recommended.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>The fish feed on micro growth in the aquarium. In addition, they accept fine dry and frozen food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/curculionichthys-sabaji-2/",
      "html": "<p>The animals are completely peaceful.</p>"
    }
  ],
